/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The teeth lie at the helix angle ψ to the shaft axis; 15° to 25° is usual (some syllabi write β).
   - mt = mn / cos ψ, d = mt z = mn z / cos ψ mt is the transverse module in mm and d the pitch diameter in mm.
   - Gears on parallel shafts must have opposite hands: one right-hand, one left-hand.
   - Cut a helical tooth by the normal plane.
   - Sb = σb b mn Y (Y for zv) Sb is the beam strength in N, b the face width in mm (often 10mn to 15mn).
   - Buckingham's dynamic load and the wear strength take the helix angle into account:
   - Fd = 21v(Ceb cos²ψ + Ft) cos ψ / (21v + √(Ceb cos²ψ + Ft)) with the symbols of the spur gear check; then Feff = Cs Ft + Fd.
   - with Q = 2z2/(z1 + z2) and K = 0.16(BHN/100)² for a steel pair.
   - The overlap ratio b sin ψ/(πmn) counts how many axial pitches fit in the face width.
   - The tooth force has three components.
*/
