/* book exam traps: Using z instead of zv = z/cos γ for the form factor and for Q′. | Taking the torque at the large-end radius. The load acts near the mean radius, which is smaller, so the true Ft is larger; the large-end radius makes the design unsafe. | Leaving out (1 − b/R) in the beam strength, or making b larger than R/3: the extra face is small, weak tooth near the apex. | Adding the pitch angles to 180°. They add to the shaft angle, 90° for a right-angle pair. | Swapping the force components: for the pinion the axial force has sin γ1 and the radial force cos γ1. */
