/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Let the worm have z1 starts and the wheel z2 teeth.
   - The worm's pitch diameter is not m z1.
   - So a small q gives a slender worm and a large lead angle; a large q gives a thick, stiff worm and a small lead angle.
   - The worm's pitch line velocity is v1 = πd1n_1/60 (d1 in m).
   - η = (cos αn − μ tan λ)/(cos αn + μ cot λ) η is the efficiency with the worm driving and αn the normal pressure angle (often 20°).
   - The drive is self-locking when the wheel cannot turn the worm, roughly when λ < φ (more exactly when μ > cos αn tan λ).
   - The worm's tangential force Ft1 = 2T1/d1 equals the axial thrust on the wheel.
   - A small worm box often has strong teeth and still overheats, so for continuous duty the heat check usually decides the catalogue power.
*/
