/* book Worked example 2: Q: The drive of the first example takes 2 kW. Take μ = 0.03 and αn = 20°. Find the efficiency, the wheel torque, the forces on the worm, and whether the drive is self-locking. For the bronze wheel σb = 80 N/mm² and b = 40 mm: check bending. The housing has 0.6 m² of surface, k = 15 W/(m²K) in still air and 25 W/(m²K) with a fan, air at 35 °C: find the oil temperature.
   1. Efficiency: η = (0.93969 − 0.03 × 0.1)/(0.93969 + 0.03 × 10) = 0.93669/1.23969 = 0.756. (The simple form tan λ/tan(λ + φ) with φ = 1.718° gives 0.767.)
   2. Worm torque: T1 = 2000 × 60/(2π × 1440) = 13.26 N·m; Ft1 = 2 × 13.26/0.050 = 530.5 N (this is also the wheel's axial thrust).
   3. Wheel torque: T2 = ηP/ω2 = 0.756 × 2000/(2π × 36/60) = 400.9 N·m; Ft2 = 2 × 400.9/0.200 = 4009 N (the worm's axial thrust).
   4. Separating force: Fr = 530.5 × 0.34202/(0.93969 × 0.09950 + 0.03 × 0.99504) = 181.4/0.12335 = 1471 N.
   5. Self-locking: φ = tan⁻¹0.03 = 1.72° is less than λ = 5.71°, so the wheel can drive the worm back. The drive is not self-locking and needs a brake on a hoist.
   6. Bending: mn = 5 × cos 5.711° = 4.975 mm, Y = 0.484 − 2.87/40 = 0.4123, Sb = 80 × 40 × 4.975 × 0.4123 = 6563 N. v2 = π × 0.200 × 36/60 = 0.377 m/s, Cv = 6/6.377 = 0.941, Ft2/Cv = 4260 N; factor 6563/4260 = 1.54.
   7. Heat: loss = (1 − 0.756) × 2000 = 489 W. Still air: t − t0 = 489/(15 × 0.6) = 54.3 K, oil at 89 °C, too hot. With a fan: 489/(25 × 0.6) = 32.6 K, oil at 68 °C, acceptable.
   Answer: η = 75.6 %, T2 = 400.9 N·m, Ft1 = 530.5 N, Ft2 = 4009 N, Fr = 1471 N; not self-locking; bending factor 1.54; the oil reaches 89 °C in still air, so fit a fan (68 °C). */
