/* book exam traps: Taking d1 = m z1. The worm is a screw: d1 = qm. | Reversing the ratio: i = z2/z1, not z1/z2. | Putting the pressure angle in place of the friction angle in η = tan λ/tan(λ + φ). | Forgetting the starts: L = z1πm, so a two-start worm has twice the lead and a larger lead angle. | Self-locking means λ < φ, roughly; it brings an efficiency below 50 % and is not a sign of good design. Swapping the thrusts: the worm's axial thrust equals the wheel's tangential force. */
